## Handle “Unknown”, “Prefer not to answer”, and Missingness (Pre-Imputation).

- For missing PCC symptoms, assume the subject did not experience that specific symptom. 

- For unknown/prefer not to answer/missing “antiviral_dayX”, treat as a single “Unknown” category.
    + Out of 10 days, if there is at least one antiviral_dayx == “yes”, assign category “Yes” for antiviral medication;
    + If there is no “yes” and there is at least one antiviral_dayx == “Unknown”, assign category “Unknown” for antiviral medication;
    + If there is no “yes” or “Unknown” (which means all antiviral_dayx == “No”), assign category “No” for antiviral medication.

- For unknown or prefer not to answer prior COVID-19 infection (“prior_covid”), combine to be a single “Unknown” category. 

- For missing COVID-19 vaccine (“covid_vaccine_verify_received”, Nmiss=10), use the pre-verification variable “vaccine_covid_doses” to fill in the missing values.
   + 8 out of 10 subjects have 2 or 3 doses recorded, assigned category “Yes”;
   + 1 subject (study_id 140305) is “Don't know / don't remember”, assign category “Unknown”; 
   + 1 subject (study_id 404201) is “No doses, have not received vaccine”, assign category “No”.
   + When combine prior infection and vaccine into one variable (“vaccine_infection”), assign category “Unknown” if any of these two variables is “Unknown”.

- For unknown/prefer not to answer/missing daily symptoms, treate as no symptoms on that day.

- For unknown/missing comorbidities (there was no “prefer not to answer” in all responses), assume no comorbidity for that 1 subject under age 21 (study_id: 400903, age at enrollement: 14.9 yrs), and use single imputation for others (see details of remaining unknown/missing comorbidities and imputation in Section 6).


