# Latent Class Analysis (LCA)

We also adopted a traditional clustering method, LCA, as a complementary analysis.

## Setup and Implementation - Different Seeds and Number of Classes

- Tried 10 different seeds
- With each seed, run LCA model with 15 different numbers of classes (ranging from 1 to 15)
- Report 4 measures: BIC, AIC, $G^2$, and $\chi^2$. 
    + Among the measures, BIC is regarded as the most reliable measure and being reported most. 
    + According to @weller2020latent, *"Although there is no consensus about the best criteria for comparing latent class solutions, there is some agreement that to select a final model (a) multiple fit statistics should be used (or at least reported); (b) the Bayesian information criterion (BIC) may be the most reliable fit statistic and should routinely be reported [@nylund2007deciding, @vermunt2002latent]; and (c) theoretical interpretability should be considered in choosing a solution [@muthen2000development, @nylund2007deciding, @shanahan2013large, @stringaris2013irritable]*; and *"The most reported is the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC; @killian2019systematic). Some researchers consider it the most reliable indicator of model fit [@nylund2007deciding, @vermunt2002latent]."*
    + For a larger dataset, AIC is more likely to select a more complex model in comparison with BIC 
    + $G^2$ and $\chi^2$:
      - Both of them are goodness-of-fit test statistics, measuring the discrepancy between the fitted model and observed data. And both have an approximate $\chi^2$ distribution under the null hypothesis (i.e., no difference between model and observed data). We will reject null hypothesis when Chi-square test statistic is big, indicating the model does not fit the observed data. Therefore, a **smaller** $G^2$ or $\chi^2$ value indicating a **better** fit.
      - They are not typically used in the selection of number of classes. since *"comparisons of latent class models do not meet certain regularity assumptions for a traditional LR difference test with a $\chi^2$ distribution (Aitkin & Rubin, 1985)."* *The fit statistics suggested to be used are adjusted likelihood tests (i.e., Vuong-Lo-Mendell-Rubin adjusted likelihood ratio test (Lo et al., 2001; Vuong, 1989) and the bootstrapped likelihood ratio test (McLachlan & Peel, 2000). These statistics provide a p value, which indicates if one model is statistically better than another." Entropy is another diagnostic statistic (Wang et al., 2017), it indicates how accurately the model defines classes.*
    
    
- For the LCA model:
    + Included all the covariates as indicators (though did not include the outcome "any_symptom_fu90")
    + Choose 10000 as the maximum number of iterations through which the estimation algorithm was set to run (maxiter=1e4)

